Task: AsyncApiChannelsProvider trait + reflective-loading test (Phase 1, Tasks 1-2 of sbt-asyncapi-plugin_20261017)

Summary: Added AsyncApiChannelsProvider, a trait a consuming service's
singleton object implements to expose its registered AsyncApiChannel list.
This is the contract the sbt plugin's future AsyncApiSpecWriter (Task 3) will
load reflectively by fully-qualified class name, so purekafka never needs a
compile-time dependency back onto the consuming service.

Files:
- modules/purekafka/src/main/scala/purekafka/docs/AsyncApiChannelsProvider.scala (new)
- modules/purekafka/src/test/scala/purekafka/docs/AsyncApiChannelsProviderSuite.scala (new)

Why: Step one of replacing the shell-script AsyncAPI generation flow
(generate-kafka-asyncapi-docs.sh) with a real sbt plugin that any
consuming service can add and auto-chain to `compile`.

Deviation: spec.md's reflection snippet (`Class.forName(name).getField("MODULE$")`)
needed a `$`-suffixed class name — Scala compiles a plain top-level `object`
into both a module class (name + "$", holding the real MODULE$ field) and a
separate static-forwarder "mirror" class (plain name, no MODULE$ field).
`Class.forName(name)` resolves the mirror class, so `getField("MODULE$")`
threw NoSuchFieldException until the test was fixed to look up `name + "$"`.
Same fix will be needed in AsyncApiSpecWriter's own reflection call (Task 3).

TDD: Red confirmed via `sbt "pureKafka/testOnly purekafka.docs.AsyncApiChannelsProviderSuite"`
(compile failure: "Not found: type AsyncApiChannelsProvider"), then Green
after implementing the trait and fixing the reflection bug above. Full
`pureKafka/test` suite (35 tests) passes; scalafmt clean.
